    <desc>Show or hide the scrollbar, scroll on output or keystrokes, and limit the scrollback.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Scrolling</title>

<p>The scrolling settings belong to the profile:</p>
  <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item><item><p>In the <gui>Profiles</gui> group, click the profile you want to change. Its settings open on a page of their own.</p></item>
<item><p>Change the settings in the <gui>Scrolling</gui> group.</p></item>
</steps>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Show scrollbar</gui></title>
      <p><gui>Regular</gui> always shows the scrollbar next to the text,
      <gui>Overlay</gui> shows it over the text while you scroll, and
      <gui>None</gui> hides it. The marks of
      <link xref="cmd-shell-integration#marks">commands</link> are shown next
      to the scrollbar.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Kinetic scrolling</gui></title>
      <p>Keep scrolling for a moment after you lift your fingers from a
      touchscreen.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Scroll on Output</gui></title>
      <p>Scroll to the end when a program prints something, so that the
      newest output is always shown.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Scroll on Keystroke</gui></title>
      <p>Scroll to the end when you press a key.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Scroll on Paste</gui></title>
      <p>Scroll to the end when you paste text.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <section id="lines">
    <title>Scrollback lines</title>
    <p>The scrollback holds the lines that have scrolled out of view. Turn on
    <gui>Limit Scrollback</gui> and set <gui>Scrollback Lines</gui> to keep at
    most that many lines, or turn it off to keep all of them. A large
    scrollback uses more memory.</p>
  </section>

